    confidence: 'medium', reviewedAt: '2026-09-26',
    claim: 'The National Assembly’s official text of the 1991 Constitution establishes Bulgaria’s formal parliamentary-republic framework, but does not establish the practice or political meaning of the 2025–26 transition. The Central Election Commission’s Decision 4807-NS (23 April 2026) allocates 131 of 240 seats to Progressive Bulgaria, 39 to GERB–SDS, 37 to We Continue the Change–Democratic Bulgaria, 21 to the Movement for Rights and Freedoms, and 12 to Revival; its open-data portal publishes the updated machine-readable results database. The Council of Europe Parliamentary Assembly’s 18 June observation report identifies this as Bulgaria’s eighth early parliamentary election since April 2021. The OSCE/ODIHR–PACE preliminary findings judged the vote transparently and efficiently administered and described a genuine voter choice, while also identifying gaps in the legal framework, a last-minute change to polling places abroad, intense polarization and persistent allegations of vote buying and intimidation; these are attributed observer findings, not a verdict on every constituency or the whole political order. Parliament’s official record and the State Gazette establish that on 8 May the 52nd Assembly elected Rumen Radev prime minister and approved the cabinet; the vote was 124 in favour, 70 against and 36 abstentions. AP’s reporting and ODIHR’s needs-assessment record connect the December 2025 resignation to large protests over the draft budget and longer-running corruption/governance concerns, without proving a single motive shared by all protesters or voters. Crampton’s national history traces political, social and economic development through EU accession; Bell’s edited study provides a distinct contemporary account of the post-1989 transition; Vachudova’s comparative book analyses early transition competition and subsequent EU enlargement leverage. These book-length works deepen institutional context but predate the current episode and do not explain the 2025 protests, Radev’s coalition, voter motives or the 2026 result; no book-length scholarship on the 2026 election itself was located. Across the six interpretive dimensions, this bounded record shows: economic grievances and budget/tax proposals as a documented protest trigger, but no basis for assigning one economic doctrine to the electorate or inferring cabinet outcomes; increased electoral participation alongside unresolved questions about electoral access and reported vote-buying risks, without treating turnout as consent to a single programme; constitutional parliamentary succession, caretaker administration, election management and majority cabinet formation alongside observer-identified legal and campaign concerns, rather than a simple “stable/unstable” classification; national and anti-corruption rhetoric as contesting political claims, not a singular Bulgarian identity; the election’s European and foreign-policy context—including recent euro adoption and debate over relations with Russia, as summarized by IPU Parline—without evidence here of a completed foreign-policy reorientation; and no sufficient episode-specific evidence to characterize religious institutions or voters as directing the result. The period is therefore best read as a protest-influenced parliamentary realignment with both formal electoral continuity and documented process concerns, not as proof of a completed democratic consolidation, a settled ideological shift or a representative national preference.',
    limitation: 'A dated Bulgarian political-history case bounded to the December 2025 protests and cabinet resignation, the caretaker period, 19 April 2026 early parliamentary election and 8 May cabinet formation, reviewed on 26 September 2026. It is not an assessment of later government performance, the 25 October 2026 presidential election, or a score or ideological profile for Bulgaria, Bulgarians, protesters, voters, Progressive Bulgaria, Radev, other parties, minorities, religious communities, regions or institutions. The Constitution records formal design; CEC records establish legal results; Parliament and the State Gazette establish office-holding decisions; and ODIHR/PACE report observer findings from a defined mission, not universal experience or a final account of all litigation and electoral irregularities. AP and IPU provide secondary context and data synthesis. Three book-length works provide independent historical/comparative framing but do not cover this episode; event-specific monographs, party and protest archives, Bulgarian-language local research, disaggregated participation and motive data, court decisions, campaign-finance audits, post-election implementation and later 2026 developments remain open research gaps. No claim is made that the majority vote equals unanimity, that a reported anti-corruption mandate has been implemented, or that one political label explains the result.',
    sourceIds: ['bulgariaConstitution1991Parliament', 'bulgariaCecElection2026', 'bulgariaCecOpenData2026', 'bulgariaOsceNeedsAssessment2026', 'bulgariaOscePreliminary2026', 'bulgariaPaceObservation2026', 'bulgariaParliamentConstitutingSession2026', 'bulgariaParliamentPrimeMinisterElection2026', 'bulgariaStateGazettePrimeMinister2026', 'bulgariaCramptonHistory2005', 'bulgariaBellTransition1998', 'bulgariaVachudovaEuropeUndivided2005', 'bulgariaApElection2026', 'bulgariaIpuElection2026'],
