    claim: 'ONPE’s official report anchors Peru’s 2021 presidential, congressional and Andean Parliament election process; the PUCP edited volume interprets the eighteen-candidate first round, the Castillo–Fujimori runoff, pandemic conditions and a crisis of representation. The official 7 December 2022 congressional resolution records the legislature’s declaration of Pedro Castillo’s permanent incapacity and vacancy and applies the constitutional succession framework; its statement that Castillo attempted to dissolve Congress is the legislature’s legal characterization of the event, not by itself a neutral adjudication or proof of public consent. The Defensoría del Pueblo’s 2023 report documents the political crisis and protest sequence, its regional and legal dimensions and the human-rights framework, while Boluarte’s 28 July 2023 address records the executive’s own account of succession, security and governance. Aragón and Sánchez provide a book-length Spanish-language analysis of power, state–citizen relations and perceived legitimacy; Costa and Romero interpret the Castillo period through electoral polarization, governance failure, corruption and competing authoritarian temptations; and Barrenechea and Vergara’s edited study supplies a distinct broader account of Peru’s post-2001 democratic deterioration and representation crisis. On six axes: economically, the 2021 contest joined rural and redistributive or state-capacity demands to market-oriented and continuity projects, while the later official agenda foregrounded order, investment and governance; these sources do not establish one settled economic doctrine or uniform policy implementation. Socially, pandemic inequality, rural and Andean marginalization, regional protest, public security, detention and rights claims were central, but official and book-length accounts do not represent every locality, language or social group. On authority, the constitutional succession mechanism, the attempted dissolution, Congress’s vacancy action, executive–legislative conflict, emergency and protest governance formed a rapidly changing institutional sequence rather than one stable regime. On identity, national republican citizenship, provincial and rural belonging, Andean and Indigenous claims, anti-elite and anti-Fujimori memories, conservative order appeals and competing national-popular projects interacted without one speaking for all Peruvians. External orientation is not sufficiently documented here for a full foreign-policy placement; the case records only the interaction of domestic crisis with regional and international legal or diplomatic settings. Religious-axis evidence is likewise insufficient for a precise match: moral and religious actors may matter, but the selected constitutional and political record is formally republican and plural rather than clerical. These are dated records and source-attributed interpretations, not numerical scores or claims that Peru, Peruvians, Castillo, Boluarte, Congress, protesters, a class, region, ethnicity, language, party, church, security institution or later government represents one ideology.',
    limitation: 'A bounded Peruvian political history from the 2021 election through the 2024 governance horizon, not a current-country claim or a score for Peruvians, Quechua, Aymara, Amazonian and other Indigenous peoples, regions, rural or urban residents, women, men, classes, workers, parties, Castillo, Boluarte, Congress, the police, armed forces, churches, courts, protesters or any later government. ONPE establishes an election process; Congress establishes its own vacancy resolution; the Defensoría establishes an institutional human-rights and protest record; the presidential address is executive self-description; and the books have different periods, genres, methods, languages and standpoints. None by itself proves the constitution’s uniform implementation, the legality or factual completeness of every institutional claim, protester or security-force responsibility in every locality, public consent, judicial independence, social distribution, Indigenous inclusion, gender equality or policy causation. Quechua-, Aymara- and Amazonian-language scholarship, regional and local histories, fuller Congress, court, prosecutor, police and military records, independent accountability findings, migration, labor, gender and social-movement histories, and developments after 2024 remain open research tasks.',
