    relationship: 'implemented',
    actor: 'Emmerson Mnangagwa, ZANU-PF and the government’s Second Republic project; the Zimbabwe Defence Forces and other security institutions; Parliament, courts, the Zimbabwe Electoral Commission and Human Rights Commission; opposition parties and candidates; traditional leaders, churches, civil society, media, women, persons with disabilities, workers and communities affected by land, economic and regional policy',
    historicalSetting: 'Zimbabwe’s November 2017 removal of Robert Mugabe, the 2018 and 2023 harmonised elections, the Mnangagwa government’s Second Republic claims and constitutional/devolution debates, and the 2026 Constitution Amendment No. 3 process, gazetted Act 6 of 2026, competing referendum interpretations and reported Constitutional Court procedural challenge',
    startYear: 2017,
    endYear: 2026,
    periodLabel: '2017–2026 · post-Mugabe transition, Second Republic, electoral contestation and constitutional-amendment dispute',
    datePrecision: 'approximate',
    evidenceKind: 'official constitutional, electoral and human-rights records; two independent book-length perspectives ending in 2023; 2026 parliamentary bill and debate, gazetted Act record, attributed civil-society/legal analyses, and a dated report of a procedural court hearing; event-specific book-length scholarship and a merits judgment remain unverified',
    confidence: 'medium',
    reviewedAt: '2026-09-26',
    claim: 'The 2013 Constitution records Zimbabwe’s formal constitutional order, rights, Parliament, courts, devolution, electoral commission and security institutions, but formal design does not establish uniform implementation, civilian control or public consent. The Zimbabwe Electoral Commission’s 2018 report and 2023 annual report provide election-administration witnesses; the Zimbabwe Human Rights Commission supplies a distinct 2018 rights-monitoring record. For 2023, the European Union Election Observation Mission and Zimbabwe Election Support Network provide international and domestic observation perspectives with different mandates. Moyo and Helliker’s edited volume examines the claimed Second Republic through political liberties, transitional justice, reconciliation, corruption, media, traditional leadership, devolution, development, debt and social policy; Mavengano and Chirongoma’s edited volume focuses on the 2023 election through gender, disability, media, religion, language and electoral-politics chapters; Mlambo supplies broader national-history context. These are book-length perspectives ending in or before 2023, not studies of the later amendment. Parliament’s 2026 Amendment No. 3 Bill proposed replacing direct presidential election with election by a joint sitting of Parliament and extending presidential and parliamentary terms from five to seven years; it also proposed changes to electoral administration, senators and constitutional commissions. The bill memorandum’s continuity/stability rationale is the sponsor’s stated purpose, not evidence of effects. The subsequent legal record identifies the Constitution of Zimbabwe (Amendment) Act (No. 3), Act 6 of 2026, gazetted 7 July. Publication as an Act is distinct from whether disputed provisions were validly enacted, have legal effect or apply to incumbents. Parliament’s Hansard records one member’s argument that a referendum was not required; the Law Society of Zimbabwe’s submission and Veritas’s later analysis argue that provisions trigger the constitutional referendum procedure. These are attributed, competing legal positions—not a judgment. A September 23 newspaper report says the Constitutional Court reserved judgment on applicants’ eligibility for direct access in a challenge; the reported procedural question is not a merits ruling. Hofisi’s International IDEA ConstitutionNet analysis is an attributed pre-enactment critique of the Bill and predates both the Act and litigation. No merits judgment or authoritative resolution of the disputed application was identified in this review. Across six axes, the 2017–23 books and institutional records support a mixed, contested account: economic policy combines land and liberation legacies, debt and development claims without proving outcomes; social rights and inclusion coexist with unequal implementation and representation gaps; authority is distributed among executive, party, Parliament, courts, electoral bodies, security institutions, opposition and civil society, while the 2026 proposals would alter presidential selection and term architecture if effective; identity claims around liberation, sovereignty, citizenship, land and language remain plural and contested; external orientation is only partially evidenced through sanctions, regional diplomacy, debt and observation; and Christian, customary and traditional-leader authorities enter public debate within a formally non-clerical constitutional order. The six-axis reading is interpretive, bounded to these records and periods, not numerical scoring, legal adjudication or a claim that Zimbabweans or any party, community or institution share one ideology.',
    limitation: 'A bounded Zimbabwean political history from the November 2017 transition through the 2023 harmonised election and the 2026 Amendment No. 3 / Act 6 legal dispute, not a current-country claim or a score for Zimbabweans, Shona, Ndebele, other language or regional communities, rural or urban residents, women, men, persons with disabilities, workers, war veterans, churches, traditional leaders, ZANU-PF, opposition parties, the military, courts, ZEC, ZHRC, observers or any later government. The Constitution establishes formal design; ZEC and ZHRC records represent specific institutions; EU and ZESN reports have distinct mandates; Hansard documents arguments rather than legal correctness; the Act record establishes Gazette publication rather than validity or application; Veritas and the Law Society offer attributed legal positions rather than judgments; and the cited court report concerns the threshold for direct access, not merits. Hofisi’s analysis predates passage. The two 2023 books have distinct scopes and neither is an event-specific monograph on the 2026 amendment. No conclusion is made about whether the Act is in force, applies to incumbents, validly bypassed a referendum, or will produce the proposed institutional effects; no merits ruling was identified in the sources consulted as of this review. Uniform rights implementation, civilian control, electoral fairness, public consent, policy causation, economic recovery, devolution, land distribution, gender or disability equality, and every locality’s experience are not established by this record. Shona-, Ndebele- and other-language scholarship, local and oral histories, military and party archives, fuller transition and 2018 violence records, judicial records and full merits litigation, event-specific book-length scholarship, land and customary-authority research, women’s, labor, disability and youth histories, additional regional observation, and developments after 26 September 2026 remain open research tasks.',
    sourceIds: ['zimbabweConstitution2013Parliament', 'zimbabweZec2018HarmonisedElection', 'zimbabweHumanRightsCommission2018', 'zimbabweZecAnnualReport2023', 'zimbabweEuElectionObservation2023', 'zimbabweZesnElectionReport2023', 'zimbabweMoyoHellikerSecondRepublic2023', 'zimbabweMavenganoChirongomaElectoralPolitics2023', 'mlamboHistoryZimbabwe2014', 'zimbabweCAB3Bill2026Parliament', 'zimbabweCAB3Hansard2026', 'zimbabweConstitutionAmendmentAct6-2026', 'zimbabweVeritasCAB3Validity2026', 'zimbabweLawSocietyCAB3Submission2026', 'zimbabweConCourtCAB3AccessSeptember2026', 'zimbabweHofisiCAB3InternationalIDEA2026'],
    locator: 'Parliament of Zimbabwe Constitution; ZEC and ZHRC records; EU EOM and ZESN 2023 reports; Moyo–Helliker, Mavengano–Chirongoma and Mlambo book records; Parliament CAB3 Bill and Hansard; Gazette Act 6/2026 legal record; Veritas and Law Society legal arguments; The Herald court-procedure report (23 September 2026); Hofisi’s pre-enactment International IDEA commentary.'
