    labelId: 'ecuadorian-noboa-security-referendum-and-reelection-order',
    placeId: 'ecuador',
    relationship: 'implemented',
    actor: 'Daniel Noboa and the Acción Democrática Nacional government; the executive, armed forces and police; the National Assembly, Constitutional Court, CNE and TCE; Luisa González, Revolución Ciudadana and other opposition parties; Indigenous, labor, business, regional, local and civil-society actors; and transnational security, financial and diplomatic institutions',
    historicalSetting: 'Ecuador’s 2023 early presidential and legislative election and Noboa’s interim presidency; the January 2024 executive security decree; the 2024 and 2025 referendum processes and 2025 presidential election; and 2026 Constitutional Court conditional review and compliance of a proposed CPCCS appointment amendment, alongside the CNE’s call for a separate future local/CPCCS election on 29 November 2026',
    startYear: 2023,
    endYear: 2026,
    periodLabel: '2023–2026 · early election, security-centered executive rupture, referendum politics, presidential reelection, conditional CPCCS amendment procedure and scheduled local/CPCCS election',
    datePrecision: 'approximate',
    evidenceKind: 'official constitutional, executive, referendum, compliance and electoral records triangulated with five independent book-length political, Indigenous-history, populism and constitutional-history perspectives; direct book-level scholarship on the full 2023–2026 sequence and the amendment’s eventual outcome remain open',
    confidence: 'medium',
    reviewedAt: '2026-09-26',
    claim: 'The 2008 Constitution establishes Ecuador’s formal rights, plurinational, intercultural, secular and presidential framework, while the 2023 CNE record anchors the early election that brought Noboa to the interim presidency. Executive Decree 111 records the government’s January 2024 addition of an internal-armed-conflict basis to the state of exception and mobilization of security institutions; it is a primary executive self-description, not an independent legal or human-rights finding. CNE results document the 2024 referendum and consultation, the 2025 presidential second round and May transfer, and the November 2025 referendum endpoint. In a distinct 2026 constitutional procedure, the Court’s 19 March Dictamen 9-25-RC/26A conditionally approved the wording of Noboa’s proposed referendum question on transferring CPCCS authority-designation powers to the National Assembly, requiring a textual correction and immediate Court compliance review before an executive call. The Court’s April compliance bulletin says the corrected annex was verified, the earlier dictamen declared complied with, and the referendum procedure authorized to continue. This completed a procedural condition; it did not enact the amendment, call a referendum or supply a referendum date or result. Separately, on 31 July the CNE called the local and CPCCS elections for 29 November 2026, still a future election as of this review; this is not the proposed constitutional referendum. Freidenberg and Pachano supply a systematic Spanish-language account of Ecuadorian institutions, parties, electoral systems and Indigenous and regional actors; Pachano offers a distinct comparative democratic-quality and left-turn lens; Becker reconstructs Indigenous and Indigenous-leftist movement history; de la Torre supplies a focused populism and political-culture account; and Blanksten adds a historical constitutional perspective. Those five books have different periods and scales and none is a book-length study of the full 2023–2026 sequence. The six-axis interpretation retained here is therefore dated to the earlier institutional record and scholarship: it describes a mixed and contested political economy, constitutional rights and plural identities alongside emergency power and unequal implementation, multiple institutional and movement actors, intersecting civic/Indigenous/regional/class/gender/party identities, external relationships and a formally secular, religiously plural constitutional order. The 2026 procedural updates do not independently justify extending that interpretation. These are bounded, attributed records and interpretations—not numerical scores or claims that Ecuador, Noboa, ADN, Correísmo, any voter, region, party, security institution, Indigenous nation or later government represents one ideology.',
    limitation: 'A bounded Ecuadorian political-history case from the 2023 early election through 2026 court procedure and a future election schedule, not a live ideological profile or a score for Ecuadorians, Kichwa, Shuar, Afro-Ecuadorian or other peoples, regions, classes, genders, parties, churches, security institutions, Indigenous organizations, voters, Noboa, ADN, Correísmo or any later authority. The Constitution records formal design; the decree records executive action and self-classification; Court records establish legal procedure; CNE records establish official electoral administration and scheduled events. None alone establishes independent observation, equal campaign conditions, voter motivation, consent, human-rights outcomes, implementation or future results. The November 2026 local/CPCCS election had not occurred by this review date. The CPCCS appointment amendment had judicial permission to continue after correction, but the sources cited here do not establish a CNE call, date, vote or result for that separate referendum. The six-axis interpretation remains anchored in the earlier case record and does not purport to characterize the later procedural developments. The five selected books span different periods and methods; none studies the full 2023–2026 sequence. Kichwa- and other Indigenous-language scholarship, Amazonian/coastal/local histories, labor, gender, migration, Afro-Ecuadorian and disability research, security and prison records, independent election observation, constitutional and legislative practice, and the amendment’s eventual status and outcome remain open research tasks.',
    sourceIds: ['ecuadorConstitution2008Asamblea', 'ecuadorCneElection2023', 'ecuadorExecutiveDecree1112024', 'ecuadorCneReferendum2024', 'ecuadorCneElection2025', 'ecuadorCneReferendum2025', 'ecuadorCourtCpccsReferendumReview2026', 'ecuadorCourtCpccsCompliance2026', 'ecuadorCneLocalCpccsElectionConvocation2026', 'ecuadorFreidenbergPachanoPoliticalSystem2016', 'ecuadorPachanoGiroPolitico2021', 'ecuadorBeckerIndiansLeftists2008', 'ecuadorDeLaTorrePopulistSeduction2000', 'ecuadorBlankstenConstitutions2022'],
