    reviewedAt: '2026-09-22',
    claim: 'The 1999 Constitution preserves Venezuela’s formal republican, participatory and rights-based design, including an electoral branch and recall mechanisms, but formal design does not establish institutional independence, public consent or equal political access. The Carter Center’s final observation record for the 28 July 2024 presidential election documents serious transparency and observation constraints and does not treat the announced outcome as established by precinct-level publication. The UN High Commissioner for Human Rights report covers 1 May 2024–30 April 2025, while the Independent International Fact-Finding Mission report covers the election, post-election period and 25 May 2025 parliamentary and regional elections through 31 August 2025; their distinct mandates document rights violations, repression, detention and civic-space concerns and note that most opposition forces did not participate in the May elections, while not replacing Venezuelan court, party, electoral or community records. Corrales’ institutional account, López Maya’s Spanish-language political history, Smilde and Hellinger’s edited volume, Ellner’s class and popular-politics study and Hawkins’ analysis of Chavismo provide independent perspectives on democratic backsliding, rentier and partisan crisis, participation, class, social movements, organization and populist ideas. On six axes, the bounded evidence supports a mixed and contested reading: oil dependence, crisis management, social programs and market or sanction pressures interact economically without one settled doctrine; constitutional social rights and participatory claims coexist socially with repression, detention, migration, class, regional, gender, Indigenous and Afro-Venezuelan inequalities; executive, party, electoral, judicial, military, security, opposition and civic institutions interact with disputed checks and constrained public space; Bolivarian sovereignty, anti-oligarchic and anti-imperial claims interact with partisan, class, regional, Indigenous, Afro-Venezuelan, religious, gendered and migrant identities without one speaking for all Venezuelans; external orientation is shaped by international observation, sanctions, United States conflict, regional diplomacy and transnational migration but is not a complete foreign-policy history; and Catholic, Protestant, secular, Indigenous and Afro-Venezuelan religious or cultural actors matter within a formally plural constitutional order rather than a clerical state. These are dated institutional records and source-attributed interpretations, not numerical scores or claims that Venezuela, Maduro, Chavismo, the opposition, a party, the military, an electorate or any community represents one ideology.',
    limitation: 'A bounded Venezuelan political-history case from the 28 July 2024 presidential election through the 25 May 2025 parliamentary and regional elections and UN reporting through August 2025, not a current-country or current-government claim, a final judgment on disputed legitimacy, or a score for Venezuelans, Chavistas, opposition parties, regions, classes, Indigenous, Afro-Venezuelan, religious, gendered, migrant or diaspora communities, the presidency, National Assembly, courts, armed forces, CNE, social movements, observers or any later authority. The Constitution establishes formal design; the Carter Center documents an international observation record; the two UN reports have distinct mandates and periods; and the books offer different languages, methods, periods and standpoints. None proves uniform rights implementation, electoral fairness, final results, public consent, judicial independence, civilian control, social outcomes, economic causation, casualty totals or the experience of every locality. Venezuelan-language and Indigenous/community scholarship beyond the selected records, regional and local histories, electoral and court archives, party and military records, women’s, labor, disability and religious histories, migration and diaspora evidence, independent observation beyond the Carter Center and developments after August 2025 remain open research tasks; no 2026 status is asserted.',
    sourceIds: ['venezuelaConstitution1999Gaceta', 'venezuelaCarter2024Election', 'venezuelaUnFactFindingMission2025', 'venezuelaUnOhchr2025', 'venezuelaCorralesAutocracyRising2023', 'venezuelaLopezMayaOcaso2016', 'venezuelaSmildeHellingerBolivarianDemocracy2011', 'venezuelaEllnerRethinking2008', 'venezuelaHawkinsChavismo2010'],
    locator: '1999 Constitution; Carter Center final report on the 2024 presidential election; UN A/HRC/59/58 and A/HRC/60/61; Corrales, López Maya, Smilde–Hellinger, Ellner and Hawkins book records.'
