    claim: 'The 2011 Constitution anchors formal constitutional-monarchical institutions, rights, identity and judicial/legislative language, while the 2021 election portal documents the 8 September legislative, regional and municipal electoral sequence and official seat-distribution record rather than equal competition or public consent. The Government Programme 2021–2026 and Ministry of Economy and Finance record frame social-protection expansion and Framework Law No. 09-21 as central policy commitments; those records establish planned/official objectives and launch, not completed universal coverage, equal outcomes or independent implementation assessment. Miller and Waterbury provide long-run and elite-centered accounts of palace authority and political field; Tozy gives a French-language sociology of sharifian legitimacy, the Makhzen and Islamist rupture/continuity; Zeghal, Boukhars and Madani add Islamist-electoral, reform and French-language constitutional-protest perspectives. On six axes, the bounded evidence supports a mixed reading: market and developmental political economy coexists with social-protection and public-provision commitments; formal rights, representation and inclusion claims coexist with uneven implementation, gender, class, regional and Amazigh questions; palace/Makhzen authority interacts with the government, Parliament, parties and territorial institutions; Moroccan sovereignty, Arab and Amazigh identity, religious-monarchical legitimacy and regional belonging interact without one speaking for all Moroccans; external orientation is not fully reconstructed here and must be kept distinct from Western Sahara and other foreign-policy records; and Islam’s constitutional/religious role and customary or secular actors matter within a monarchy rather than clerical government. These are dated institutional records and source-attributed scholarly interpretations, not numerical scores or claims that Morocco, the monarchy, a party, the coalition, a religion, region or community represents one ideology.',
    limitation: 'A bounded Moroccan case from the 8 September 2021 elections through the 2021–2026 programme horizon, with research ending in 2025; not a current-2026 government claim or a score for Moroccans, Arabs, Amazigh, Sahrawis, regions, classes, genders, religious or secular communities, parties, voters, Parliament, the monarchy, Makhzen institutions or social-protection beneficiaries. The Constitution and official election/policy records document formal design, administration and stated commitments; they do not prove equal competition, parliamentary autonomy, judicial independence, completed universal coverage, fiscal sustainability, social distribution, gender equality, Amazigh or regional inclusion, public consent or the lived experience of every household. The selected books cover different periods and standpoints rather than the entire 2021–2025 sequence; Arabic and Amazigh scholarship, party and parliamentary archives, independent election observation, implementation and beneficiary data, labor and women’s histories, local and regional variation, Western Sahara legal/territorial records and all developments after 2025 remain open research tasks; no live 2026 status is asserted.',
