    confidence: 'medium',
    reviewedAt: '2026-09-26',
    claim: 'The 1947 Constitution anchors Japan’s formal popular-sovereignty, rights-bearing and parliamentary-cabinet order. The official June 2026 Diet committee record reports that the 20 July 2025 House of Councillors election returned 125 members, turnout was 58.51%, and the LDP–Komeito governing side won 47 elected seats and lacked majorities in both houses. It reports that in the 8 February 2026 House of Representatives election, the LDP won 315 of 465 seats and the Japan Innovation Party 36; IPU Parline separately records one independent joining the LDP after the vote and the resulting 316-member affiliation, so elected party result and subsequent alignment are kept distinct. The Kantei records Takaichi’s designation on 21 October 2025, her stated rationale for dissolution and the February election, and the reshuffled cabinet’s September 2026 agenda; these executive statements establish official self-description, not policy effects or voter motives. A Japanese-language multi-author volume edited by Chiki Ogiue analyzes the 2024 and 2025 elections but predates the 2026 contest; Mikuriya and Serikawa’s September 2026 dialogue addresses Takaichi and the Reiwa cabinets but is a current political-history dialogue, not a peer-reviewed monograph. Suk’s Korean-language journal study and Lipscy’s Pacific Affairs article offer distinct contemporary interpretations of Takaichi’s leadership, ideology, mobilization and governing position; Pajon’s IFRI paper supplies separate policy analysis. These sources identify interpretive claims, not settled causal findings. Dower’s occupation history, Gordon’s national history, Curtis’s institutional study and Jansen’s long modern history remain essential book-length context, not accounts of this full 2021–2026 sequence. Six-axis reading is deliberately cautious: the case combines capitalist/developmental political economy with welfare and redistributive commitments; formal rights and elected inclusion with persistent gender, class, minority, migrant and regional questions not resolved by constitutional text; cabinet, Diet, courts and election administration with party, faction and coalition bargaining; civic and symbolic national frameworks with plural Ainu, Okinawan, Zainichi, migrant, regional and partisan histories; alliance dependence, Article 9 and current debate over defense policy, without treating announced proposals as implemented; and a formally non-establishment state with diverse religious and customary influences. The primary record supports electoral and institutional chronology, not numerical ideological scores, uniform public preference, or a claim that any party, group or government represents all Japan.',
    limitation: 'A bounded case from the 2021 lower-house election through records available 26 September 2026; not a timeless country or people profile, a judgment on the 2026 government’s performance, or a score for Japan, Japanese people, Ainu, Okinawans, Zainichi, migrants, women, youth, workers, regions, voters, parties, LDP factions, Komeito, the emperor, Cabinet, Diet, courts, bureaucracy, Self-Defense Forces or later policy. Official results establish dated seats and turnout, not voter motives, campaign fairness, equal access, consent or the causes of party shifts. Kantei statements document the executive’s own rationale and announced agenda; neither promises nor parliamentary strength establish implementation. The 2026 edited/dialogue books and journal/policy analyses improve contemporary coverage but do not constitute two book-length scholarly studies of the whole sequence; party and faction archives, campaign-finance enforcement, Japanese local and prefectural histories, Okinawan and Ainu political histories, Zainichi and migrant citizenship, gender, labor, disability, youth, religion, media and election observation remain research tasks. Six-axis interpretations are source-attributed and provisional, not a measured population-level distribution.',
    sourceIds: ['japanPostwarConstitution1946', 'japanHouseElectionResults2021', 'japanHouseElectionResults2024Ipu', 'japanPoliticalReformKishida2024', 'japanPoliticalFundsLaw2024Diet', 'japanHouseCouncillorsElection2025', 'japanHouseElectionOfficialReport2026', 'japanHouseElectionChronology2026', 'japanTakaichiDesignation2025', 'japanTakaichiDissolutionPressConference2026', 'japanTakaichiCabinetSeptember2026', 'japanHouseElectionResults2026Ipu', 'ogikamiElectionBook2026', 'mikuriyaSerikawaReiwaCabinets2026', 'sukTakaichiAdministration2026', 'lipscyTakaichiAbePacificAffairs2026', 'pajonTakaichiLandslideIfri2026', 'dowerEmbracingDefeat1999', 'gordonModernJapan2002', 'curtisLogicJapanesePolitics1999', 'jansenMakingModernJapan2000'],
