colombiaCase.claim += ' The Registraduría’s 2026 calendar fixes congressional voting on 8 March, the presidential first round on 31 May and a runoff on 21 June; its election portal distinguishes the informative, nonbinding pre-count from legally effective scrutiny. The CNE’s online record lists resolution E-3181 of 2026, published 24 June, as declaring the presidential election and ordering credentials, but its linked full document was not accessible for direct inspection. Canal Capital and El País independently report that the CNE scrutiny declared Abelardo de la Espriella and José Manuel Restrepo elected, with 12,960,166 votes against 12,708,312 for Iván Cepeda and Aída Quilcué, a reported difference of 251,854. These candidate totals are secondary reports of the official scrutiny, not a substitute for the uninspected E-26 act. The reports disagree on the number of unmarked ballots (28,666 versus 20,666), and Canal Capital labels the CNE resolution E-381 rather than the CNE listing’s E-3181; ballot-category totals and the numbering discrepancy are therefore left unresolved. The EU observation mission’s first-round statement reports campaign-security incidents, threats and violence brought to or observed by interlocutors, and weaknesses in real-time political-finance oversight; allegations are attributed and are not treated as judicial findings. The OAS mission’s first-round report describes its observation sample and reports generally orderly polling/counting alongside isolated incidents; complaints recorded by the government’s monitoring center are not themselves verified findings. On 24 August the Council of State reported admitting one annulment action and denying an interim suspension, while stating that the alleged psychological violence and its effect on electoral will required evidence and had not been decided on the merits. The later docket and disposition of that case were not verified. This 2026 evidence extends the authority, social-values and civic-identity axes through a particular competitive election and scrutiny process; it does not establish fraud, uniform campaign conditions, voter motives, or one national ideological position.';
colombiaCase.limitation += ' The 2026 update is a bounded election-cycle extension, not a score or representative account of Colombia or Colombians. The CNE listing metadata and two press reports do not replace direct inspection of the signed E-26 scrutiny act: the linked CNE document could not be retrieved, secondary sources differ on unmarked ballots, and the CNE listing/reference numbering differs from one report. The EU and OAS reports cover defined observation missions, not every polling place, territory or voter; observer-reported allegations and government complaint logs are not findings of liability. The Council of State release describes an interlocutory stage only; subsequent docket activity and merits judgment were not verified. No 2026 event-specific book-length scholarly analysis was located; the existing monographs provide historical context, not evidence of 2026 results or campaign conditions. Complete congressional result/candidate-level files, campaign-finance settlements, full election-observation coverage, the judicial expediente and later rulings, and further Spanish-language and regional/community scholarship remain open research tasks.';

